"The Sky and the Celestial Sphere | VirtualDemos",
"The Sky and the Celestial Sphere",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> A winter night at Ruston; North Pole; On the equator; Southern sky, Sydney; June solstice Sun; December solstice Sun; March equinox Sun; Midnight Sun; The Moon through a month; Total solar eclipse, {#}; Total lunar eclipse, {#}; Pole star in {#} BC; Pole star in AD {#}; Precession of the pole.",
"The left view shows the sky as a sphere seen from outside, with you at the center standing on a flat green horizon. It can be shown flat or in 3D, and dragged to turn. The right view shows the same sky from the ground: facing one direction above the horizon, as a map of the whole sky overhead, or as a star chart in right ascension and declination.",
"Choose the place (or set any latitude and longitude), the year, the date and the clock time. Play turns the Earth at {#} minute, {#} minutes or {#} hour each second, steps one day each second at the same clock time, or steps {#} years each second.",
"Click a star, the Sun, the Moon or a celestial pole to select it, or find it by name. The sidebar gives its constellation, right ascension and declination, altitude and azimuth, its highest and lowest altitude in a day and whether it rises and sets. It also gives the sky as a whole: the time, the Sun’s altitude, the Moon’s phase, the star nearest the north celestial pole and any eclipse.",
"The stars are the {#},{#} stars of the Yale Bright Star Catalogue down to magnitude {#}, about what you see from a dark site. Times are standard time with no daylight saving. The real air bends light near the horizon and lifts everything there by about half a degree; the demo leaves that out.",
"Record reading stores the selected object and everything about it. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data tables below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table. Each part goes into its own table.",
"Use a model of the sky as a great sphere around Earth to explain why the stars circle the pole star, why the height of the pole star tells you your latitude, which stars never set, how the Moon moves among the stars in a week, and how the pole star changes over thousands of years.",
"<strong>The celestial sphere.</strong> The stars are so far away that they all look equally distant, as if fixed to the inside of a huge sphere around Earth. Earth turns once a day from west to east, so the whole sphere seems to turn the other way, from east to west, about an axis through Earth’s poles. The points on the sky right above Earth’s North and South Poles are the north and south celestial poles. Halfway between them, right above Earth’s equator, is the celestial equator.",
"<strong>Two ways to give a position.</strong> Altitude (the angle above the horizon) and azimuth (the compass direction, {#}° north, {#}° east, {#}° south, {#}° west) say where to look from where you stand, right now. Right ascension and declination are fixed on the sphere, like longitude and latitude on Earth: declination is the angle north (+) or south (−) of the celestial equator, and right ascension runs eastward around the sky from {#} h to {#} h.",
"<strong>Your latitude and the pole.</strong> Standing at the North Pole, the north celestial pole is straight overhead. At the equator it sits on the northern horizon. In between, the north celestial pole stands as many degrees above your northern horizon as your latitude. Polaris lies within {#}° of the pole today, so it barely moves.",
"<strong>Circumpolar stars.</strong> Every star circles the pole once a day. A star close enough to the north celestial pole never dips below the horizon: it is circumpolar. A star close enough to the south celestial pole never rises at all. Seen from latitude φ north, a star of declination δ reaches its highest altitude when it crosses the meridian (the line from north through the point overhead to south): {#}° minus the difference between φ and δ, taking the difference as positive. A star of declination {#}° seen from {#}° N, for example, gets as high as {#}° − {#}° = {#}°.",
"<strong>The Sun and the Moon.</strong> Earth’s axis is tilted {#}° to its orbit, so the Sun’s yearly path among the stars, the ecliptic, is tilted {#}° to the celestial equator. The Moon moves eastward among the stars much faster, once around the sky in {#} days, always within about {#}° of the ecliptic.",
"<strong>Precession.</strong> The pull of the Sun and the Moon on Earth’s equatorial bulge makes Earth’s axis swing slowly around a cone, like a spinning top, once in about {#},{#} years. The celestial poles move slowly among the stars, so the pole star changes.",
"Predict the altitude of Polaris seen from the North Pole, from Ruston ({#}° N) and from the equator.",
"From Ruston, what is the smallest declination a star can have and never set? (Hint: for a star to stay up all day, its distance from the north celestial pole must be less than the pole’s altitude.)",
"The Moon goes once around the sky ({#}°) in {#} days. About how many degrees does it move among the stars each day? Right ascension runs {#} h around the sky, so {#} h of right ascension is {#}°. About how much does the Moon’s right ascension grow each day?",
"Make a guess before you look: was Polaris always the pole star, for example when the pyramids at Giza were built about {#} BC?",
"No hazards in the virtual lab. If you go outside to compare with the real sky, stay somewhere safe, and never look at the Sun.",
"<strong>Polaris from different latitudes.</strong> Choose A winter night at Ruston (January {#}, {#}, {#}:{#} pm) with Polaris selected. Record a reading at Ruston, the North Pole, Tromsø, Giza, Quito and Sydney, then at latitudes {#}° N, {#}° N and {#}° N at Ruston’s longitude. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Stars and the Sun from Ruston.</strong> At Ruston, record a reading for Kochab, Dubhe, Capella, Vega, Betelgeuse, Sirius, Antares and Canopus. Then choose June solstice Sun and record the Sun, and choose December solstice Sun and record the Sun again. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>The Moon through a week.</strong> Choose The Moon through a month (Ruston, January {#}, {#}, {#}:{#} pm) with the Moon selected. Record the Moon at {#}:{#} pm on each of the seven evenings from January {#} to January {#}. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
